course: dictionary
lesson: 2098
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words change their meaning depending on where you meet them. «Continental» is one of these. Its meaning often depends on the words next to it, so read the whole phrase, not just the one word.
explain: In geography, «continental» means to do with a continent, one of the world's large land masses. «A continental climate» has hot summers and cold winters, and it is far from the sea. In «continental drift», the big pieces of land move slowly over time.
explain: In everyday talk in the UK and Ireland, «continental» means from mainland Europe, as opposed to Britain and Ireland. A hotel offers a «continental breakfast», and a family might go on «a continental holiday». In the US, «the continental United States» means the main part of North America, which is all states except Alaska and Hawaii.
explain: To choose the right meaning, ask: what is the phrase about? A breakfast or a holiday points to food or travel. Crust, shelf and drift point to rocks and the land under the sea. Then test your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Continental is an adjective, and it describes a noun such as breakfast, climate or crust.
fact: «Continental» can mean to do with a continent, one of the world's large land masses.
fact: In the UK and Ireland, «continental» often means from mainland Europe.
fact: A «continental breakfast» is bread, croissants, jam, cheese and coffee, not a cooked breakfast.
fact: Continental plates move slowly, and they can cause earthquakes.
exercise: A hotel menu offers «a continental breakfast». What is it? | Soup and rice | Eggs, bacon and sausages | *Bread, croissants, jam, cheese and coffee instead of a cooked breakfast | A packed lunch for a school trip
exercise: «The deepest hole ever drilled reached about 12 km, not even halfway through the continental crust there.» What does continental tell you about the crust? | It is under the sea only | It is covered in ice all year | It is from mainland Europe, and nothing else | *It belongs to a continent, one of the world's large land masses
exercise: Ella reads «a continental holiday» in a travel advert. Where might the holiday be? | *Mainland Europe, away from Britain and Ireland | The Moon | Antarctica | Her own back garden
test: In the US, «the continental United States» means what? | The sea around the coast | *All states except Alaska and Hawaii | Only Alaska and Hawaii | Every country in Europe
test: «When two continents collide, neither sinks, because continental crust is too light, so the crust crumples upwards into huge mountains.» What does the crust crumple into? | Small islands | Deep holes | *Huge mountains, such as the Himalayas | Flat, sandy beaches
exam: Which sentence uses «continental» to mean from mainland Europe? | «Continental drift moves the land very slowly.» | «The continental climate has cold winters.» | «The continental shelf lies under the sea.» | *«We booked a continental holiday in Italy.»
